\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We investigate how action-relevant structure is distributed across a VLA by combining four complementary analyses: cross-instruction probes, attention pattern analysis, RSA, and functional probes.

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Model and Data}
\label{sec:model_data}

We study OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024}, a generalist robot policy chaining three components: a frozen DinoSigLIP fused vision backbone~\cite{zhai2023_siglip,oquab2024_dinov2} (DINOv2 ViT-L/14 + SigLIP-SO400M), an MLP projector (2{,}176-D $\to$ 4{,}096-D), and a Llama-2 7B language backbone (6.74B parameters) that autoregressively decodes 7-dimensional actions.
We use the base checkpoint pretrained on Open X-Embodiment (no task-specific fine-tuning) and evaluate on LIBERO-Goal~\cite{liu2023_libero} (10 tabletop tasks, 50 demonstrations each).
To isolate the LLM's contribution, we construct an untrained control by reinitializing all LLM parameters via \texttt{from\_config()} (6.74B random weights), preserving the frozen vision encoder and projector architecture.

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Representation Extraction}
\label{sec:repr_extraction}

We extract representations from six sources spanning the VLA pipeline:
(a)~\textbf{DinoSigLIP+Projector}---projector output (4{,}096-D), immediately before LLM processing;
(b)~\textbf{LLM image\_mean}---mean image-token activation at each of 32 layers;
(c)~\textbf{LLM readout}---activation at the final pre-action token position at each layer;
(d)~\textbf{DINOv2 ViT-L/14}~\cite{oquab2024_dinov2} (\texttt{cls\_token}/\texttt{patch\_mean}), a self-supervised baseline;
(e)~\textbf{CLIP ViT-L/14}~\cite{radford2021_clip} (\texttt{patch\_mean}), a VL-pretrained baseline;
(f)~\textbf{Branch-split}---DINOv2 (1{,}024-D) and SigLIP (1{,}152-D) branches before fusion.
We cross two factors: \emph{training status} (trained vs.\ untrained) and \emph{instruction presence} (with vs.\ without task description) in a $2\times 2$ factorial design.

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Cross-Instruction Probes}
\label{sec:cross_instruction_probes}

To test whether different token types encode instruction identity, we construct a cross-instruction dataset: each visual frame is paired with all 10 task instructions, producing 10 representation vectors per frame per layer.
A linear classifier (10-way) is trained to identify which instruction was present, separately for image\_mean and readout token positions.
We validate with leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV) over 10 folds, each holding out one task's instructions, to confirm that results reflect generalizable instruction encoding rather than memorization.

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Attention Pattern Analysis}
\label{sec:attention_analysis}

We analyze L1 attention distributions at the readout token position across all 32 heads, categorizing each head as instruction-dominant ($>50\%$ attention to instruction tokens) or image-dominant.
This complements the cross-instruction probe by revealing the \emph{mechanism} through which instruction information reaches the readout token.

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Representational Similarity Analysis}
\label{sec:rsa}

We adopt RSA~\cite{kriegeskorte2008} to quantify geometric alignment between representations and the action space.
For each task, we compute pairwise $N \times N$ representational dissimilarity matrices (RDMs) using cosine distances for representations and Euclidean distances for 7-D action vectors, using within-task observation pairs from 50 demonstrations.
Alignment is measured via Spearman $\rho$ between the upper-triangular entries of the representation and action RDMs; higher $\rho$ indicates that representationally similar observations require similar actions.
We report mean $\rho$ across 10 tasks with 95\% confidence intervals from 10{,}000 bootstrap resamples.

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Functional Action Probes}
\label{sec:probes}

RSA measures geometric alignment but not decodability.
We complement it with MLP probes~\cite{alain2017}: two-layer networks ($d_\text{in} \to 256 \to 7$) trained to predict 7-D action vectors from frozen representations (Adam, lr${}=10^{-3}$, batch 256, 50 epochs, early stopping patience 5).
We report mean $R^2$ across action dimensions, with bootstrap paired comparisons (10{,}000 resamples) for statistical inference.
CKA~\cite{kornblith2019} results are reported in Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We evaluate OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024} on LIBERO-Goal~\cite{liu2023_libero} (10 tasks, 50 demonstrations each).
Representations are extracted from six sources spanning the VLA pipeline (\S\ref{sec:repr_extraction}): DinoSigLIP+Projector, LLM image\_mean and readout tokens at each of 32 layers, DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024_dinov2} (\texttt{cls\_token}/\texttt{patch\_mean}), CLIP~\cite{radford2021_clip} (\texttt{patch\_mean}), and branch-split (DINOv2 1{,}024-D / SigLIP 1{,}152-D before fusion).
The $2\times 2$ factorial design crosses training status (trained vs.\ untrained LLM, reinitializing 6.74B parameters) with instruction presence (\S\ref{sec:model_data}).
RSA computes Spearman $\rho$ between cosine-distance representation RDMs and Euclidean-distance action RDMs, with 10{,}000 bootstrap CIs (\S\ref{sec:rsa}).
MLP probes (input $\to$ 256 $\to$ 7; Adam, lr${}=10^{-3}$, 50 epochs, early stopping) report mean $R^2$ (\S\ref{sec:probes}).

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Token-Level Functional Specialization}
\label{sec:token_specialization}

We test whether image tokens and readout tokens encode different types of information by training cross-instruction probes (\S\ref{sec:cross_instruction_probes}): given the same visual frame paired with each of 10 task instructions, we classify which instruction was present from per-layer activations.

\paragraph{Image tokens are instruction-blind.}
At the image\_mean position, instruction classification remains at chance ($10\%$) across all 32 layers, in both trained and untrained models (Table~\ref{tab:dense_routing_main}).
This result holds identically for standalone DINOv2 \texttt{cls\_token} ($10\%$), confirming that instruction blindness is a property of the visual representation itself, not an artifact of the VLA pipeline.
Language instructions do not modulate the geometric structure of image representations: across all conditions, instruction presence produces a maximum $|\Delta| = 0.006$ in RSA, well within noise (Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}, panel b).

\paragraph{Readout token as multimodal hub.}
The readout token (last\_preaction) tells a starkly different story.
Trained last\_preaction achieves $100\%$ 10-way classification accuracy from L1 through L31 (Table~\ref{tab:dense_routing_main}).
Leave-one-out cross-validation over 10 folds (each holding out one task's instructions) confirms perfect generalization: $100\% \pm 0.00$ across all folds at L1--L31 (Appendix~\ref{sec:loocv}).
The untrained model also achieves near-perfect accuracy at L1 ($100\%$) but progressively decays: $95.5\%$ at L8, reaching $82\%$ by L31.

\paragraph{Functional implications.}
This strict division of labor---instruction-blind image tokens and instruction-conditioned readout token---means the readout token is the sole locus of multimodal integration within the VLA.
QA probes for task progress and subtask classification confirm that the readout token encodes richer task-relevant information than vision-only representations: trained last\_preaction achieves $86.3\%$ (L20), exceeding DINOv2 \texttt{cls\_token} ($82.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:qa_probe}).

