%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Training Learns Deliberate Instruction Routing}
\label{sec:learned_routing}

The cross-instruction probe reveals \emph{what} each token type encodes; attention analysis reveals \emph{how} instruction information reaches the readout token.

\paragraph{Attention reorganization at L1.}
We analyze per-head attention distributions at L1 for the readout token position (Figure~\ref{fig:attention}).
In the trained model, 25 out of 32 attention heads are instruction-dominant: they allocate on average $58\%$ of attention weight to instruction tokens (${\sim}8\%$ of the sequence) and only $20.9\%$ to image tokens.
In the untrained model, 0 out of 32 heads are instruction-dominant: attention defaults to image tokens ($87.8\%$) with only $11.7\%$ allocated to instruction tokens.

\paragraph{Deliberate vs.\ incidental routing.}
Both models achieve near-perfect instruction classification at L1 because instruction embeddings from the frozen tokenizer are inherently highly distinguishable: even $12\%$ incidental attention provides enough signal for a linear probe.
The critical difference is robustness.
Trained deliberate routing (58\% attention, 25/32 dedicated heads) creates a signal strong enough to survive 30 subsequent layers ($100\%$ at L31).
Untrained incidental routing ($12\%$ attention, 0/32 dedicated heads) produces a fragile signal that degrades to $82\%$ by L31.
Training thus does not enable routing---architecture provides incidental leakage---but transforms it from fragile to robust by learning a fundamentally different attention strategy.

\paragraph{Asymmetric training effects.}
This routing mechanism explains why training effects are concentrated at the readout token rather than image tokens: training reshapes how the readout token attends to instruction tokens (attention $+46$pp), maintains routing through depth ($+18$pp at L31), and improves functional decodability ($R^2$ $+0.069$), while image\_mean metrics change minimally ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:training_effect_summary}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig8_attention.pdf}
\caption{L1 attention from the readout token, per head (sorted by instruction attention fraction). \textbf{(a)}~Trained: 25/32 heads are instruction-dominant ($>50\%$ attention to instruction tokens, blue). \textbf{(b)}~Untrained: 0/32 heads are instruction-dominant; nearly all attention goes to image tokens (orange). Training learns deliberate instruction routing, creating a robust signal maintained through L31 ($100\%$) vs.\ untrained decay ($82\%$).}
\label{fig:attention}
\end{figure}

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Non-Monotonic Refinement of Vision Encoder Output}
\label{sec:nonmonotonic}

With token-level specialization and routing established, we characterize how the LLM transforms the geometric structure inherited from the vision encoder.

\paragraph{Vision encoder provides geometric foundation.}
Table~\ref{tab:rsa_overview} reports RSA $\rho$ between each representation source and the action space.
Self-supervised DINOv2 \texttt{cls\_token} achieves the highest alignment ($\rho = 0.692$), exceeding the fused DinoSigLIP ($0.634$), the VLA's own peak ($0.628$ at L12), and CLIP ($0.557$; Figure~\ref{fig:rsa_bar}).
This ordering---self-supervised $>$ VL-pretrained---is consistent with behavioral evidence of visual dominance~\cite{grant2026}.
Functional probes corroborate: DINOv2 ($R^2 = 0.634$) $\geq$ Projector ($0.630$, $p = 0.557$, n.s.) $>$ CLIP ($0.587$, $p < 0.001$).

\paragraph{Inverted-U pattern.}
Figure~\ref{fig:layer_curves} reveals a non-monotonic trajectory for trained image\_mean RSA: $\rho$ begins at $0.612$ (L0), rises to $0.628$ at L12 (above the Projector baseline $0.615$), then declines to $0.422$ at L32.
The untrained control peaks at L0 ($0.612$) and decays monotonically to $0.545$.
Full-layer probe scans confirm the inverted-U in functional capacity: trained image\_mean $R^2$ peaks in the L8--L10 region ($0.655 \pm 0.002$ across seeds; Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_sensitivity}), exceeding the projector ($0.630$, $\Delta = +0.025$).
The readout token concentrates a functionally superior representation: trained readout achieves $R^2 = 0.678$ (L13), exceeding the projector by $\Delta = 0.048$ (Table~\ref{tab:rsa_overview}, Figure~\ref{fig:probe_bars}).

\paragraph{Training redistributes geometry, enhances decodability.}
Training shifts the image\_mean RSA peak from L0 to L12 with a modest best-vs-best gain ($\Delta = +0.017$, $3\%$; Table~\ref{tab:training_effects}; Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}).
LIBERO-Spatial confirms with $\Delta = +0.023$, $4\%$, $p < 0.001$ (\S\ref{sec:cross_benchmark}), suggesting the LIBERO-Goal CI (incl.\ zero) reflects limited power.
$R^2$ best-vs-best shows larger improvements: image\_mean $\Delta = +0.015$ ($p = 0.006$), readout $\Delta = +0.069$ ($p < 0.0001$).
At L12, training produces a large same-layer effect ($\Delta = +0.079$, $p < 0.0001$), confirming that training has a dramatic effect on \emph{where} geometry peaks but only a modest effect on \emph{how high}.
Untrained image\_mean $R^2$ drops below zero at L10--L11 ($R^2 = -0.33$, $-0.28$); training converts this pathological zone into the network's peak.

\paragraph{Cross-benchmark validation (LIBERO-Spatial).}
\label{sec:cross_benchmark}
The inverted-U replicates on LIBERO-Spatial (10 out-of-distribution tasks; Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}): trained image\_mean RSA peaks at L14 ($\rho = 0.691$), $R^2$ at L13 ($0.696$), with peaks shifting ${\sim}2$ layers deeper---consistent with increased task difficulty.
RSA--$R^2$ peak convergence does not replicate (6-layer gap vs.\ $|\Delta_\text{layer}| = 1$ on Goal), suggesting this alignment may be benchmark-specific.
These findings are robust to distance metric choice, hold across all action dimensions, survive permutation testing, and replicate under halved demonstration counts (RSA profile $r = 0.970$; Appendix~\ref{sec:robustness_appendix}).

